The usual fix for a coding agent that knows too little is a bigger context window. Load the whole rules folder, the skills folder and the project notes, and let the model sort it out. We tried that shape first in our own sessions. It works until the library grows. Past a few hundred items, the prompt is mostly material that does not apply to the task in front of the agent, and the model is slower and less focused.

agent-brain takes the other route. Each turn, it selects a small set of items and puts a hard limit on how many tokens they may use. This post explains how that selection works, what it costs, and what it leaves out.

## The shape of one route

When the host calls `route_task`, agent-brain does four things in order.

1. **Retrieves candidates.** A keyword search over the indexed items (skills, rules, agent definitions, memories and workflows) runs in SQLite's FTS5 engine, which ranks matches with BM25. A vector search runs over the same items. Both return ranked lists, and the two lists are merged with reciprocal rank fusion. Exact matches on a symbol name get a boost.
2. **Scores each candidate.** The merged score is adjusted for the item's type, for whether it applies to the current project, and for a few signals from past runs: items that helped recently rank a little higher, and items that were followed by failures rank a little lower.
3. **Fills a budget.** The candidates are walked in score order. Each one is added only if it fits in the remaining tokens. If a memory is wanted, 120 tokens are held back for it first, so rules and skills cannot crowd every memory out.
4. **Returns the packed result**: the items chosen, the tokens used and the budget. The route is also logged, so the dashboard can count it later.

The budget is set by the kind of task. In our code, a verification request gets 350 tokens, a review gets 550, an implementation gets 600, a debugging request gets 650, and an architecture request gets 700. These apply to the default request. A caller that asks for a specific budget gets that budget.

## Why a budget and not a bigger window

A budget makes the cost of each turn known before the model sees it. The alternative, letting the host load everything, has a cost that grows with the library. The team pays for every token on every turn, and the model has more to ignore.

The budget also forces a choice. Something has to lose. We decided that a small set of relevant items is better than a large set of mostly irrelevant ones, and that a person who wants more can ask for a larger budget explicitly.

## What it costs

The budget has three real costs, and we would rather name them than hide them.

- **Things get left out.** A rule that matters for an edge case can rank below the cut. The route output lists what was chosen, so a person can see the cut and raise the budget. We do not claim that a budget finds everything.
- **Scoring is heuristic.** The weights were tuned on our own sessions and our test cases. A different codebase may need different weights. The routing regression suite has 250 cases, and it checks that the right kinds of items come back for each query. It does not prove the ranking is best for your repository.
- **Two searches cost time.** Keyword and vector search both run on each route. Our CI benchmark measured a warm route at 3 ms, but that run used a deterministic test embedder, not the production model, so it does not describe a real route. We have not yet published a latency for the default model. A cold start also loads the model, which takes longer, so the first route after a restart is the slow one.

## The vector index

The vector side uses the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model through the fastembed library. Vectors are stored as 8-bit scalar codes, one byte per dimension plus a scale per vector. That takes about 384 bytes per 384-dimension vector, against about 1.5 kilobytes for 32-bit floats. The price is a little precision.

For small indexes the search is brute force, which is simple and exact enough. For larger indexes it uses a single-layer graph with extra links per filter. We call that an HNSW-style index in our docs, but it is not the full multi-layer HNSW structure from the paper. We chose the simpler version because it was enough for the index sizes we see, and we have not measured it against the full structure at large scale.

## What we would change

The scoring weights are the part we trust least. We would like them to learn from the outcomes that verification records, and right now they only use a small, fixed set of signals. That is the next piece of work in this area.

If you want to see the routing for your own project, run `agent-brain briefing` or ask the host to call `route_task` and read what comes back. The output names the items and the budget it used.
